"the new manager directly in the child class, as that would override the "
"default and you would have to also explicitly include all the managers from "
"the abstract base class. The solution is to put the extra managers in "
"another base class and introduce it into the inheritance hierarchy *after* "
"the defaults::"
msgstr ""
"Faoi dheireadh don sampla seo, cuir i gcás gur mhaith leat bainisteoirí "
"breise a chur le rang an linbh, ach bain úsáid as an réamhshocrú ó "
"`AbstractBase ``fós. Ní féidir leat an bainisteoir nua a chur go díreach i "
"rang an linbh, mar go ndéanfadh sé sin an réamhshocrú agus bheadh ort na "
"bainisteoirí go léir ón rang bonn teibí a áireamh go sainráite freisin. Is é "
"an réiteach ná na bainisteoirí breise a chur i mbunaicme eile agus iad a "
"thabhairt isteach san ordlathas oidhreacht* tar éis * na réamhshocruithe::"

msgid ""
"Note that while you can *define* a custom manager on the abstract model, you "
"can't *invoke* any methods using the abstract model. That is::"
msgstr ""
"Tabhair faoi deara cé gur féidir leat* bainisteoir saincheaptha a shainiú ar "
"an tsamhail teibí, ní féidir leat* aon mhodhanna a úsáid ag baint úsáide as "
"an tsamhail teibí. Is é sin:"

msgid "is legal, but::"
msgstr "tá sé dlíthiúil, ach:"

msgid ""
"will raise an exception. This is because managers are intended to "
"encapsulate logic for managing collections of objects. Since you can't have "
"a collection of abstract objects, it doesn't make sense to be managing them. "
"If you have functionality that applies to the abstract model, you should put "
